<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nereid Monument</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 390–380 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nereid Monument is a temple-shaped tomb built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xanthos">Xanthos</a> in Lycia around 390–380 BCE, probably for the dynast Arbinas (Erbbina). It sets an Ionic Greek temple on a tall Lycian podium, with statues of sea nymphs (Nereids) between its columns and friezes of battle and court life. Charles Fellows shipped its remains to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in the 1840s, and it was reconstructed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nereid-monument.jpg" alt="Nereid Monument" width="320" height="291" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reconstruction_Nereid_Monument_BM.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
